{"question": "A 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension, obesity, and alcoholic cirrhosis is seen in clinic for follow-up. He feels well and currently drinks 5 glasses of wine each night. Medications include atenolol and lisinopril. On physical exam, temperature is 98.1 deg F (36.7 deg C), blood pressure is 151/82 mmHg, pulse is 71/min, and respirations are 14/min. He has spider angiomata on his chest; no asterixis, jaundice, ascites, or peripheral edema is noted. Screening ultrasound reveals a new liver nodule, and follow up CT demonstrates a 2 cm right hepatic lobe lesion with enhancement in the arterial phase. No hypodense attenuation is seen on the venous or delayed phase. What is the next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with liver biopsy", "B": "Refer for surgical resection", "C": "Refer for radiofrequency ablation", "D": "Treat with sorafenib", "E": "Observe and get follow-up imaging in 3 months"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Gastroenterology", "Hepatology", "Cardiology", "Oncology", "Addiction Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Gastroenterology", "Oncology"], "question_analyses": {"Gastroenterology": "The medical scenario presented describes a 65-year-old man with a complex medical history and a newly discovered liver lesion. Here is a detailed analysis of the case:\n\n### Clinical Context and Findings\n- **Patient History**: The patient has a history of hypertension, obesity, and alcoholic cirrhosis. He is also a heavy drinker, consuming 5 glasses of wine each night. - **Medications**: He is on atenolol and lisinopril for hypertension. - **Physical Exam**: The patient is afebrile, with a blood pressure of 151/82 mmHg, a heart rate of 71/min, and respiratory rate of 14/min. Spider angiomas are noted on his chest, which are a common finding in patients with cirrhosis due to increased levels of estrogen. No asterixis, jaundice, ascites, or peripheral edema is noted, which is reassuring but does not rule out 